# Test: an external plugin declared on the command line brings its own YANG root
#
# Question: when the config holds no `plugin` block and the external plugin is
# named with --plugin, does the daemon still take the plugin's module before it
# parses the config, and deliver the block under its root at Stage 2?
# Why not a unit test: TestFirstPassMergesCommandLinePlugins proves the merge of
# the two sources. This proves the command line reaches that merge from the
# real `ze` entry, and that the plugin it names is run before the full parse.
#
# VALIDATES: AC-16 of spec-plugin-external-yang-at-startup -- a plugin declared
#   on the command line gives the same outcome as one declared in the config.
# PREVENTS: the first pass reading plugin declarations from the config alone,
#   so a plugin named only on the command line starts after the parse and its
#   root is refused with "unknown top-level keyword".
#
# The exit assertion is of the DAEMON'S own exit. `then=stop` on the await
# makes the runner wait for daemon.ready and for the awaited line, stop the
# daemon itself, and compare the code the daemon ended with
# (docs/architecture/testing/ci-format.md, "Await", step 3). So exit 0 means
# the daemon started, ran the plugin and stopped cleanly. A plain await
# compares the peers' wait, and this file starts no peer.

stdin=ze-conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
acme-monitor {
	target probe-b
	interval 3
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze --plugin "le test fixture plugin/external-yang valid" -:stdin=ze-conf:timeout=30s
await=stderr:contains=FIXTURE: external-yang generation 1 ready:then=stop
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=FIXTURE: external-yang configure root=acme-monitor leaves=[acme-monitor/interval=3 acme-monitor/target=probe-b]
reject=stderr:contains=unknown top-level keyword
reject=stderr:pattern=ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL
